Description: libpq.pc: Drop libcurl from Requires.private
 PostgreSQL 18 added libcurl to PKG_CONFIG_REQUIRES_PRIVATE in
 src/interfaces/libpq/Makefile, because clients linking libpq.a statically
 get libpq-oauth, and libpq-oauth needs libcurl. pkgconf, however, resolves
 Requires.private even for --cflags and gives up if any listed module has no
 .pc file:
 .
   Package 'libcurl', required by 'libpq', not found
 .
 libpq-dev depends on libssl-dev but not on libcurl4-openssl-dev, so
 "pkg-config --cflags libpq" exits non-zero and prints nothing. Programs
 that use it to locate libpq-fe.h then fail to build even though the headers
 need nothing from libcurl; the autopkgtests of hoel and rust-pq-sys are two
 examples. "pkg-config --libs libpq" still works, since private dependencies
 are not expanded when linking dynamically.
 .
 The alternatives discussed on pgsql-hackers were to make libpq-dev depend
 on libcurl4-openssl-dev, or to have every libpq consumer build-depend on it
 itself. Both pull an otherwise unrelated development package into the
 dependency chain and make pkgconf emit libcurl's include flags as well.
 .
 Upstream follows the pkg-config FAQ, which recommends Requires.private for
 private dependencies precisely so that --libs --static works, and there was
 no agreement on the semantics of the proposed Requires.internal. The thread
 "libcurl in libpq.pc" ends with the issue being left to distributions to
 work around:
 https://postgr.es/m/aCHScGj9_dAo-gOM@msg.df7cb.de/
 .
 Since all libpq users in Debian link dynamically, drop libcurl from
 Requires.private and add -lcurl to the private link flags instead:
 --cflags now succeeds without libcurl4-openssl-dev, and static linkers
 still get -lcurl from --libs --static, or a meaningful "libcurl not found"
 from their own linker if it is missing.
Author: Christoph Berg <myon@debian.org>
Forwarded: not-needed

--- a/src/interfaces/libpq/Makefile
+++ b/src/interfaces/libpq/Makefile
@@ -110,10 +110,7 @@ $(call add_to_list,PKG_CONFIG_REQUIRES_P
 endif
 
 ifeq ($(with_libcurl),yes)
-# libpq.so doesn't link against libcurl, but libpq.a needs libpq-oauth, and
-# libpq-oauth needs libcurl. Put both into *.private.
-$(call add_to_list,PKG_CONFIG_REQUIRES_PRIVATE,libcurl)
-%.pc: override SHLIB_LINK_INTERNAL += -lpq-oauth
+%.pc: override SHLIB_LINK_INTERNAL += -lpq-oauth -lcurl
 endif
 
 all: all-lib libpq-refs-stamp
